window.__labLoaded({"id":"2.4","title":"Conditional Probability","goal":"Read conditional probabilities from the lesson's table of 200 students (studied or not, passed or not) and show that P(pass given studied) is not P(studied given pass).","question":"A teacher recorded whether 200 students studied for a test and whether they passed. A student asks two questions that sound alike: given that someone studied, what is the chance they passed, and given that someone passed, what is the chance they studied? Are the two answers the same, and which one tells us whether studying is linked with passing?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Build the two-way table","text":"We list the 200 students as (group, result) pairs, then pandas crosstab counts them into the same table you saw in the lesson. margins=True adds the totals.","code":"import pandas as pd\n\nrows = ([('Studied', 'Passed')] * 90 + [('Studied', 'Failed')] * 10\n        + [('No study', 'Passed')] * 40 + [('No study', 'Failed')] * 60)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=['group', 'result'])\n\ntable = pd.crosstab(df['group'], df['result'], margins=True)\nprint(table)","explain":["Load pandas, the library for working with tables of data.","Build the list of 200 (group, result) pairs by repeating each pair as many times as it occurred: 90 studied and passed, 10 studied and failed, and so on.","Continue the list with the students who did not study.","Turn the list into a table (a DataFrame) with named columns.","Count the pairs with crosstab, which makes a two-way table. margins=True adds the totals.","Print the table to see the whole picture before we calculate."],"meaning":"The table shows 200 students. Of the 100 who studied, 90 passed and 10 failed. Of the 100 who did not study, 40 passed and 60 failed. In total 130 passed and 70 failed. Everything we need is in these counts.","out":"result    Failed  Passed  All\ngroup                        \nNo study      60      40  100\nStudied       10      90  100\nAll           70     130  200","figs":[]},{"title":"P(pass given studied), two ways","text":"First restrict to the Studied row and take the share who passed. Then use the formula P(A and B) / P(B). Both routes must agree.","code":"n = len(df)\nstudied = table.loc['Studied', 'All']\nboth = table.loc['Studied', 'Passed']\n\nprint(f'row method : {both} / {studied} = {both / studied:.3f}')\nprint(f'formula     : {both / n:.3f} / {studied / n:.3f} = {(both / n) / (studied / n):.3f}')","explain":["Count all students. We need the total for the formula.","Read the number who studied from the 'All' column of the Studied row. This is the restricted group we condition on.","Read how many studied and passed. This is the 'A and B' count.","Print the row method: among the students who studied, the share who passed.","Print the formula method P(A and B) / P(B). It is the same division written in probabilities."],"meaning":"Among the 100 students who studied, 90 passed, so P(pass | studied) = 0.900. The formula gives the same: 0.450 divided by 0.500 = 0.900. Conditional probability just means narrowing the data to the people who meet the condition first.","out":"row method : 90 / 100 = 0.900\nformula     : 0.450 / 0.500 = 0.900","figs":[]},{"title":"The reverse question is different","text":"P(studied given pass) restricts to the Passed column instead. The numbers differ, so never swap the two. Normalising the table by row or by column shows both at once.","code":"passed = table.loc['All', 'Passed']\nprint(f'P(pass | studied) = {both / studied:.3f}')\nprint(f'P(studied | pass) = {both} / {passed} = {both / passed:.3f}')\n\nprint('\\nrow percentages (condition on the row):')\nprint(pd.crosstab(df['group'], df['result'], normalize='index').round(3))","explain":["Read the total number of passers, because this time the condition is 'passed'.","Print P(pass | studied) again as the baseline.","Compute P(studied | pass): among the passers, the share who studied. This is a different question.","Print a heading with a blank line before it so the table below is easy to find.","Print the table as row percentages, which condition on the row, so each row sums to 1."],"meaning":"P(pass | studied) is 0.900, but P(studied | pass) is 90 / 130 = 0.692. They are different because the groups we restrict to are different. The row percentages show 0.9 of studiers passed and only 0.4 of non-studiers passed, which is the real comparison.","out":"P(pass | studied) = 0.900\nP(studied | pass) = 90 / 130 = 0.692\n\nrow percentages (condition on the row):\nresult    Failed  Passed\ngroup                   \nNo study     0.6     0.4\nStudied      0.1     0.9","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"tab <- matrix(c(90, 10, 40, 60), nrow = 2, byrow = TRUE,\n              dimnames = list(c(\"Studied\", \"No study\"), c(\"Passed\", \"Failed\")))\naddmargins(tab)\ntab[1, 1] / sum(tab[1, ])         # P(pass | studied)\ntab[1, 1] / sum(tab[, 1])         # P(studied | pass)\nprop.table(tab, 1)                # row proportions","explain":["Build the table as a matrix: counts for Studied (passed, failed) then No study. byrow = TRUE fills row by row.","Name the rows and columns so the output is readable.","Add the row and column totals.","Pass given studied: the Studied-Passed cell over the row total.","Studied given pass: the same cell over the column total, a different denominator.","Row proportions: each row divided by its own total."],"expect":"P(pass | studied) = 0.9; P(studied | pass) = 0.6923; row proportions 0.9 0.1 / 0.4 0.6","read":"Read three numbers: 0.9 is P(pass | studied), 0.6923 is P(studied | pass), and the proportions table shows the rows 0.9 0.1 and 0.4 0.6. The two conditional probabilities differ because the denominators differ.","note":"prop.table(tab, 1) divides each row by its total, which is exactly conditioning on the row. Use prop.table(tab, 2) to condition on the column."},"spss":{"menu":"Data > Weight Cases (weight by freq); Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Crosstabs, then Cells: tick Row and Column percentages","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / group result freq.\nBEGIN DATA\n1 1 90\n1 2 10\n2 1 40\n2 2 60\nEND DATA.\nVALUE LABELS group 1 'Studied' 2 'No study'\n  /result 1 'Passed' 2 'Failed'.\nWEIGHT BY freq.\nCROSSTABS /TABLES=group BY result\n  /CELLS=COUNT ROW COLUMN.","explain":["Describe the data layout: group, result and the count of students (freq).","Start the data block.","Studied and passed: 90 students.","Studied and failed: 10 students.","Did not study and passed: 40 students.","Did not study and failed: 60 students.","End the data block.","Attach words to the codes for group, so the table is readable.","The same for result.","Weight cases by the count, so each row counts as 90, 10, 40 or 60 students.","Ask for the crosstab of group by result.","Show counts, row percentages and column percentages in each cell."],"read":"In the Crosstabulation table read the Studied row. '% within group' is 90.0% (pass given studied). '% within result' for Studied among passed is 69.2% (studied given pass). The two percentages answer different questions.","note":"In the table, % within group on the Studied row is 90.0% (pass given studied). % within result on the Passed column is 69.2% for Studied (studied given pass)."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput group result freq\n1 1 90\n1 2 10\n2 1 40\n2 2 60\nend\nlabel define g 1 \"Studied\" 2 \"No study\"\nlabel values group g\nlabel define r 1 \"Passed\" 2 \"Failed\"\nlabel values result r\ntabulate group result [fweight = freq], row column","explain":["Remove old data from memory.","Start typing data for three variables.","Studied and passed: 90 students.","Studied and failed: 10 students.","Did not study and passed: 40.","Did not study and failed: 60.","End the data input.","Define text labels for group.","Attach them to the variable.","Define text labels for result.","Attach them to the variable.","Cross-tabulate group by result using freq as a weight; row and column ask for both percentages."],"read":"In each cell the three numbers are the count, the row percentage and the column percentage. The Studied/Passed cell shows 90, 90.00 (pass given studied) and 69.23 (studied given pass). Compare the two percentages to see the difference between the two questions.","note":"Each cell shows the count, then the row percentage (90.00 for pass given studied), then the column percentage (69.23 for studied given pass)."},"conclusion":"Among students who studied, 90% passed, compared with only 40% of those who did not study, so studying goes with a much higher pass rate. The reverse probability, P(studied | pass) = 0.692, is a different question and should not be swapped in. The caution: this shows an association in 200 students, not proof that studying alone caused the passes.","practice":{"scenario":"A cafe manager counted 250 visitors: 100 came in the morning and 150 in the afternoon. In the morning, 70 bought a drink and 30 left without buying. In the afternoon 45 bought and 105 left. She asks about the chance of buying given the time of day, and the reverse.","data":"Morning: 70 bought, 30 left. Afternoon: 45 bought, 105 left. Build the 250 (visit, result) pairs with lists like [('Morning', 'Bought')] * 70.","tasks":["Make the two-way table with totals.","Find P(bought), and P(bought | morning).","Find P(morning | bought) and say why it differs from P(bought | morning)."],"hint":"Use pd.crosstab with margins=True. For a conditional probability, divide the cell by the total of the row (or column) you are conditioning on.","solution":{"code":"import pandas as pd\n\nrows = ([('Morning', 'Bought')] * 70 + [('Morning', 'Left')] * 30\n        + [('Afternoon', 'Bought')] * 45 + [('Afternoon', 'Left')] * 105)\ndf = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=['visit', 'result'])\ntable = pd.crosstab(df['visit'], df['result'], margins=True)\nprint(table)\n\nprint(f\"P(bought)             = {table.loc['All', 'Bought'] / len(df):.3f}\")\nprint(f\"P(bought | morning)   = {table.loc['Morning', 'Bought'] / table.loc['Morning', 'All']:.3f}\")\nprint(f\"P(morning | bought)   = {table.loc['Morning', 'Bought'] / table.loc['All', 'Bought']:.3f}\")","answer":"The table has 115 buyers out of 250, so P(bought) = 0.460. Given morning, 70 of 100 bought: P(bought | morning) = 0.700. Given a purchase, 70 of the 115 buyers came in the morning: P(morning | bought) = 0.609. The numbers differ because the first restricts to morning visitors and the second to buyers. Morning visitors buy more often than the 0.46 overall rate.","out":"result     Bought  Left  All\nvisit                       \nAfternoon      45   105  150\nMorning        70    30  100\nAll           115   135  250\nP(bought)             = 0.460\nP(bought | morning)   = 0.700\nP(morning | bought)   = 0.609","figs":[]}}});
